## Step 1: Mandatory reconsideration

Before you can appeal, you must ask the DWP to look at the decision again. This is called a **mandatory reconsideration**.

- You must ask within **one month** of the date on your decision letter.
- You can ask by **phone**, by **letter**, or using form **CRMR1** from GOV.UK.
- If you phone, follow it up in writing and keep a copy.

### What to say

- Say which **activities** you disagree with, and which statement you think should apply.
- Explain **why**, with examples, using the "most days" and "reliability" rules.
- Point out anything in the **assessment report** that is wrong or missing. Ask for a copy of the report if you do not have it.
- Send any **new evidence** you have.

The DWP will send you a **mandatory reconsideration notice** with its new decision.

## Step 2: Appeal to a tribunal

If you still disagree, you can **appeal** to an independent tribunal, called the First-tier Tribunal (Social Security and Child Support). It is not part of the DWP.

- You must appeal within **one month** of the date on your mandatory reconsideration notice.
- You can appeal **online** at GOV.UK, or on form **SSCS1**.
- You will need a copy of your mandatory reconsideration notice.

### The hearing

A tribunal panel is made up of a **judge**, a **doctor** and a **disability expert**. You can take part in person, by phone or by video. The hearing is less formal than a court. The panel will ask you questions about your daily life.

{% callout type="good" title="It is worth appealing" %}
Official tribunal figures show that most PIP appeals that reach a hearing are decided in the claimant's favour. Many people also have their decision changed at the mandatory reconsideration stage.
{% /callout %}

## Get help with your challenge

A challenge is much easier with help. Contact:

- **Citizens Advice**
- your council's **welfare rights** service
- a local **law centre**
- a **charity** for your condition

Next, see our [PIP points self-check](/benefits/pip/points-checker/) to check which statements you think should apply to you.
